ordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/crate/p3-sumcheck/0.8.0/source/Cargo.toml\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://docs.rs/crate/p3-sumcheck/0.8.0/source/Cargo.toml is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBatchZK: A Fully Pipelined GPU-Accelerated System for Batch Generation of Zero-Knowledge Proofs (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1862)\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view1\",\"pattern\":\"History\"}); Total lines: 84\nL47: Second, our system supports recent efficient ZKP protocols with their computational modules: sum-check protocol, Merkle tree, and linear-time encoder. We customize these modules to fit our pipelined execution. Third, we adopt a dynamic loading method for the data required for proof generation, reducing the required device memory. Moreover, multi-stream technology enables the overlap of data transfers and GPU computations, reducing overhead caused by data exchanges between host and device memory.\nL48: We implement our system and evaluate it on various GPU cards. The results show that our system achieves more than 259.5× higher throughput compared to state-of-the-art GPU-accelerated systems. Moreover, we deploy our system in the verifiable machine learning application, where our system generates 9.52 proofs per second, successfully achieving sub-second proof generation for the first time in this field.\nL49: ##### Metadata\nL50: Available format(s)\nL51:     【17†PDF】\nL52: Category\nL53:     【18†Implementation】\nL54: Publication info\nL55:     Published elsewhere. Minor revision. ASPLOS 2025\nL56: Keywords\nL57:     【19†GPU】【20†zero-knowledge proof】【21†pipeline】【22†sumcheck protocol】【23†Merkle tree】【24†linear-time encoder】\nL58: Contact author(s)\nL59:     lutao2020 @ zju edu cn\nL60: chenyuxunzju @ zju edu cn\nL61: zhwang @ zju edu cn\nL62: xiaohangwang @ zju edu cn\nL63: chenwz @ zju edu cn\nL64: jhzhang @ nus edu sg\nL65: History\nL66:     2024-11-15: approved\nL67:     2024-11-14: received\nL68:     【25†See all versions】\nL69: Short URL\nL70:     【26†https://ia.cr/2024/1862†ia.cr】\nL71: License\nL72: \nL73: 【27†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL74: BibTeX 【28†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL75: \nL76:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/1862,\nL77:           author = {Tao Lu and Yuxun Chen and Zonghui Wang and Xiaohang Wang and Wenzhi Chen and Jiaheng Zhang},\nL78:           title = {{BatchZK}: A Fully Pipelined {GPU}-Accelerated System for Batch Generation of Zero-Knowledge Proofs},\nL79:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/1862},\nL80:           year = {2024},\nL81:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1862}\nL82:     }\nL83: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.37710v1)\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"github.com\"}); Total lines: 348\nL86: The finding that programming-model overhead decreases with arithmetic width, measured over 758 validated configurations from an 800-configuration sweep on an H100, with the same trend observed on an A100: CUDA’s median speedup falls from $3.5\\times$ at 32 bits to $1.4\\times$ at 256 bits, where 30 of 171 configurations finish within $1.1\\times$ of CUDA and Triton is faster on 19.\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: Backend-independent kernel optimizations for wide-field SumCheck: specialized point interpolation replaces small-point Montgomery multiplications with cascading modular additions and speeds up 256-bit evaluation kernels by $1.65$–$1.75\\times$ in CUDA and $1.55$–$1.59\\times$ in Triton. A limb-major table layout provides a further 1–5% improvement at 256 bits when combined with specialized interpolation.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: An open, correctness-gated benchmark artifact with matched CUDA and Triton implementations of 25 prover constraints across four arithmetic widths, available at 【46†https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel†github.com】. Reported configurations require cross-backend checksum agreement, with both implementations additionally checked against a CPU big-integer reference.\nL93: ## 2. Background\nL94: ### 2.1. SumCheck as a Prover Kernel\nL95: In ZKPs, computations being proven are represented using polynomial constraints that encode the correctness of a program (【37†Setty, 2020】; 【47†Wahby et al., 2018】; 【38†Chen et al., 2023】), a process called arithmetization. The scheme populates constituent polynomials so that their evaluations at step $i$ describe the operation performed at that step, and a composite constraint polynomial vanishes across the execution domain exactly when every step executed correctly (【48†Gabizon et al., 2019】).\nL96: In the implementation, evaluations of the constituent polynomials are represented as input tables. The prover therefore needs a way to show that a polynomial vanishes over the entire execution domain without the verifier inspecting every step directly.\nL97: SumCheck provides one mechanism for this global check and appears in several proof-system constructions (【49†Lund et al., 1992】; 【50†Thaler, 2013】; 【37†Setty, 2020】). It lets a prover $\\mathcal{P}$ convince a verifier $\\mathcal{V}$ that the sum of a multivariate polynomial $f(X_{1},\\ldots,X_{\\mu})$ over the Boolean hypercube equals a claimed value $C$:\nL98: \nL99: (1)  |  | $$C=\\sum_{x_{1},\\ldots,x_{\\mu}\\in\\{0,1\\}}f(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{\\mu}).$$  |\nL100: The protocol runs for $\\mu$ rounds, one per variable $x_{i}$. In each round the prover reduces the multivariate polynomial to a univariate polynomial whose degree is bounded by the polynomial’s individual degree in the current variable $x_{i}$. The verifier checks that evaluating $x_{i}$ at $0$ and $1$ and computing their sum is consistent with the previous claim, then samples a random challenge fixing $x_{i}$ for the next round.\nL219: Table 3. CUDA–Triton runtime breakdown on H100. Entries are Triton/CUDA ratios; values below $1\\times$ favor Triton. “No kernel” is Triton’s wall-clock fraction with no GPU kernel executing.\nL220: Width, $r$  | Wall T/C  | Kernel T/C  | +tl.reduce T/C  | No kernel\nL221: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL222: 32, 20  | 4.02  | 1.38  | 0.93  | 91%\nL223: 32, 24  | 3.17  | 2.20  | 1.05  | 60%\nL224: 256, 20  | 1.52  | 1.03  | 0.92  | 49%\nL225: 256, 24  | 1.24  | 1.12  | 1.01  | 10%\nL226: ### 5.4. A Compile-Time Limit in Triton\nL227: zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp, which references 22 input tables at degree 7, completes on both backends from 32 through 128 bits. At 256 bits, CUDA still compiles the specialized-point kernels to 226–236 registers per thread without spilling, whereas Triton compilation grows from seconds at 32 bits to minutes at 64–128 bits and hours at 256 bits.\nL228: At 64 and 128 bits, 96–99% of this time is spent in TritonGPUCoalesce^{1}^{1} 1 【63†https://github.com/triton-lang/triton/blob/v3.7.0/lib/Dialect/TritonGPU/Transforms/Coalesce.cpp#L71†github.com】; the per-operation slice is built in 【64†CoalesceUtils.cpp, line 45†github.com】.. The pass repeatedly constructs def-use slices for loads and stores, causing its cost to rise rapidly as the fully unrolled kernel expands.\nL229: We observe the same behavior in the latest Triton releases, indicating a compiler scalability limit for large unrolled kernels rather than a regression specific to one version.\nL230: ### 5.5. zkPHIRE-Derived Benchmark Results\nL231: At 256 bits and $r=24$ (Table 【65†4】), CUDA retains a $1.15\\times$ geometric-mean advantage over Triton across the 24 constraints matched to zkPHIRE, although the gap varies substantially by workload family, from $1.50\\times$ for the Verifiable ASICs/Spartan constraints to $1.03\\times$ for complete addition.\nL232: The addition constraints remain the strongest case for Triton, with the two backends within three percent on complete addition, but the convergence is no longer confined to that family: addition constraints account for 34 of the 41 configurations within $1.1\\times$ and 24 of the 26 Triton wins, while several HyperPlonk configurations also approach parity.\nL233: Relative to zkPHIRE’s reported ASIC results (【45†Daftardar et al., 2026】), CUDA remains $4.60\\times$ slower in geometric-mean runtime ($74.16$ versus $16.11$ ms), leaving substantial headroom beyond what either GPU implementation recovers.\nL234: Table 4. zkPHIRE-derived constraints at 256 bits, $r=24$. Geometric-mean runtimes in milliseconds; ASIC is the zkPHIRE accelerator (【45†Daftardar et al., 2026】). Jellyfish zerocheck does not compile within the time limit at eight limbs (Section 【23†5.4】), hence $n=1$.\nL235: Family  | $n$  | Triton  | CUDA  | Speedup  | ASIC\nL236: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL237: Verifiable / Spartan  | 3  | 25.60  | 17.11  | 1.50$\\times$  | 6.31\nL238: Halo2 curve checks  | 3  | 56.21  | 41.43  | 1.36$\\times$  | 8.86\nL281:   * Farheen and Tsoutsos (2026) K. Farheen and N. G. Tsoutsos Chimera: a hybrid GPU backend for sumcheck acceleration in zero knowledge provers. Note: Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2026/1388 External Links: 【119†Link†eprint.iacr.org】 Cited by: 【120†§1】, 【121†§6】.\nL282:   * Gabizon et al. (2019) A. Gabizon, Z. J. Williamson, and O. Ciobotaru PLONK: permutations over lagrange-bases for oecumenical noninteractive arguments of knowledge. External Links: 2019/953 Cited by: 【106†§2.1】.\nL283:   * Garimella et al. (2023) K. Garimella, Z. Ghodsi, N. K. Jha, S. Garg, and B. Reagen Characterizing and optimizing end-to-end systems for private inference. In Proceedings of the 28th ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages an